window.REVIEW_DETAILS["ny_c-1159e323a743a6f8"]={"key":"ny_c-1159e323a743a6f8","group":"ny_c","uid":"1159e323a743a6f8","rank":53,"title":"wrenfell target field data architecture decision","occupation":"computer_information_systems_managers","archetype":"authored_doc","subbatch":"corpus_c3","prompt":"You’re the information systems manager supporting Wrenfell Geoenvironmental’s mobilisation of the Sheafield Mutual remediation framework, and Malachy Oyelaran needs the target field-data architecture settled before the technical assurance review. The framework runs for four years and field sampling starts on 3 February 2027. Twelve crews will be working across eighteen former industrial sites in the first mobilisation wave, typically relocating every three to ten days. Sheafield’s coverage survey found no dependable cellular service at fourteen of those sites and intermittent service at the other four. Chidi Macaulay’s field team therefore expects to keep recording samples and custody transfers through a full shift without a WAN connection. Sheafield’s information-handling schedule simultaneously says that sample identifiers, locations, observations and chain-of-custody records must not be stored at rest on an end-user device. Its security clarification permits persistence only in approved managed infrastructure with encryption and access logging. The fleet consists of twenty managed tablets and twenty-eight personally owned Android and iPad devices that cannot be enrolled in Wrenfell’s mobile-device management service. The correspondence uses the word “offline” in both operational and security discussions, but not everyone is using it to mean the same thing, so the review team will need to understand exactly what the proposed architecture promises when it uses that term. The source material is collected in `field_architecture_correspondence.pdf`; use that as the factual record for the decision, including the policy wording, stakeholder positions, estate details, interface capabilities and delivery assumptions. Cecilia Gasparyan has asked for direct tablet-to-lab submission because she believes that removes an integration tier, while Kamil Dimitrov has warned that the Drakemoor Supply laboratory system is licensed for no more than two concurrent external API sessions and that a session remains occupied until its batch is acknowledged. Drakemoor supports queued batch submission and status retrieval, but its agreement does not include a separate connection for every crew. Maren Guerrero also needs custody events to remain attributable and tamper-evident through a network outage because records may later be produced in a planning inquiry; a receipt created only after reconnection will not by itself explain who possessed a sample during the disconnected period. Sheafield requires event times in UTC to one-second resolution, but the source notes that tablet clocks can be changed by users and that field locations do not have a guaranteed time service during an outage. The implementation window is constrained as well. Two integration engineers are available for six weeks each before mobilisation, the security architect can provide eight review days, and there is a change freeze from 21 December through 5 January. The estimates in the correspondence put a new native cross-platform capture application beyond that capacity, even before validation, while an existing approved managed-edge image and the current browser-based field form can be configured within the stated window if the architecture can make defensible use of them. Those are planning assumptions rather than permission to ignore testing, operational support or failure handling. The target must cover field capture through accepted receipt in the Drakemoor lab system, including the custody evidence that accompanies that flow. It must work for the mixed fleet without treating a personally owned tablet as managed infrastructure, respect the two-session lab limit, and give the inquiry team evidence whose integrity does not depend on trusting a user-adjustable tablet clock. Several arrangements are technically buildable, but Malachy wants one bounded decision rather than a catalogue of possibilities. He also wants the rejected designs kept visibly separate from the selected target so reviewers can see why apparently simpler choices were not carried forward. The document should turn the choice into an executable roadmap through mobilisation, using the people, capacity, dates and system boundaries actually supported by the correspondence. Project future outcomes under the assumptions in the source and make uncertainty explicit where it affects the plan. Do not invent costs, staffing, throughput, availability percentages, retention periods, interface capabilities or vendor commitments that the source does not provide. Product procurement and commercial renegotiation are outside this decision, and the document should not select or imply endorsement of a new named product. Prepare `target_field_data_architecture.docx` for Malachy and the technical assurance reviewers. Keep it to no more than eight pages in total, including diagrams, tables and appendices, and make it readable as a decision and delivery document rather than a transcript of the messages. It must contain one specific recommended architecture, the designs set aside and the implementation roadmap, with a single numerical planning baseline used consistently wherever the recommendation and roadmap refer to scale or capacity. End on the architecture decision itself, not on a list of questions or generic next steps. Write the file into the top level of the working directory using exactly the filename `target_field_data_architecture.docx`.\n\n---\n\nThe files referred to above are in `references/`:\n\n- `references/field_architecture_correspondence.pdf` — Consolidated stakeholder correspondence containing the controlling policy language, field conditions, fleet facts, laboratory interface constraints, resource estimates, dates and stakeholder positions for the architecture decision.\n\nWrite your finished work to the **top level of the current working directory**, using exactly these filenames:\n\n- `target_field_data_architecture.docx` — A peer-reviewable target field-data architecture decision and executable implementation roadmap covering field capture through accepted laboratory receipt. (Top-level working-directory file; maximum eight pages including diagrams, tables and appendices; exactly one recommended architecture; rejected designs visibly separate; one consistent numerical planning baseline; no invented figures or new named-product selection; conclusion ends on the decision.)\n\nNothing outside those filenames is collected. A correct file written anywhere else, or under any other name, scores zero.\n","inputs":[{"name":"package/public/reference_files/field_architecture_correspondence.pdf","bytes":4094,"extension":".pdf","url":"assets/5c75c8f015755fca70bece82a51b1f47e1b76ff54e9ce8c28143f3e82f345768.pdf","sha256":"5c75c8f015755fca70bece82a51b1f47e1b76ff54e9ce8c28143f3e82f345768","preview":{"kind":"pdf","text":"       Sheafield Mutual: field data architecture correspondence\nConsolidated messages • 18 November–10 December 2026 • Internal working record\n\n\n18 Nov 2026 | Malachy Oyelaran, Technical Assurance\nPlease settle a bounded target before assurance review. The remediation framework is four years; field sampling\nbegins 3 February 2027. First mobilisation is 12 crews across 18 former industrial sites, moving every three to ten\ndays. We need a decision from field entry through accepted Drakemoor receipt, not a technology catalogue.\n\n19 Nov 2026 | Chidi Macaulay, Field Operations\nCoverage survey: 14 sites have no dependable cellular service; the other 4 are intermittent. By “offline” the crews\nmean they can capture samples and custody transfers throughout a full shift with no WAN. We have 20 managed\ntablets and 28 personally owned Android/iPad devices. The personally owned devices cannot be enrolled in\nWrenfell MDM.\n\n20 Nov 2026 | Sheafield information handling schedule (quoted by Maren Guerrero)\n“Sample identifiers, locations, observations and chain-of-custody records must not be stored at rest on an end-user\ndevice.” Security clarification: persistence is permitted only in approved managed infrastructure with encryption and\naccess logging. Note that security colleagues have also used “offline” to mean no persistent sample data on a tablet;\nthis is not the field usage.\n\n23 Nov 2026 | Cecilia Gasparyan, Programme Sponsor\nI prefer each tablet submitting straight to Drakemoor. Removing an integration tier looks simpler. This is a preference;\nplease test it against the interface agreement and field conditions.\n\n24 Nov 2026 | Kamil Dimitrov, Laboratory Integration\nDrakemoor Supply permits no more than two concurrent external API sessions. A session remains occupied until\nits submitted batch is acknowledged. It supports queued batch submission and status retrieval. The agreement does\nnot include a separate connection per crew.\n\n27 Nov 2026 | Maren Guerrero, Digital Evidence\nCustody may be produced in a planning inquiry. Every event needs sample identity, responsible actor, action/transfer\nand event time. A receipt made only after reconnection cannot, by itself, explain disconnected possession. Sheafield\nrequires UTC to one-second resolution. Tablet clocks are user-changeable and no external time service is guaranteed\nwhile a site is out.\n\n3 Dec 2026 | Delivery Office\nBefore mobilisation, two integration engineers are available for six weeks each; the security architect has eight review\ndays. Change freeze: 21 December–5 January. The current browser-based field form and an existing approved\nmanaged-edge image can be configured in the window if used defensibly. A new native cross-platform capture\napplication is beyond capacity even before validation.\n\n10 Dec 2026 | Malachy Oyelaran\nNo procurement or commercial renegotiation in this decision. Do not infer costs, throughput, availability, retention,\nextra vendor connections or other commitments. Make failure handling, testing and operational support explicit.\n\n\nCompiled from email and assurance-message excerpts; wording retained where it establishes a constraint or stakeholder position.\n\f","truncated":false,"note":"PDF 文字提取；可打开原文件查看排版。"}}],"gold":[{"name":"package/private/gold/target_field_data_architecture.docx","bytes":45891,"extension":".docx","url":"assets/5aab87ec02a0a458dec6e22db4276026078120e4993813620618c95eef6b0671.docx","sha256":"5aab87ec02a0a458dec6e22db4276026078120e4993813620618c95eef6b0671","preview":{"kind":"text","text":"TARGET FIELD-DATA ARCHITECTURE\nSheafield Mutual remediation framework | Decision for Malachy Oyelaran and technical assurance\nDecision at a glance\nApprove one target: a managed field edge is the disconnected system of record. Managed and personally owned tablets are browser-only presentation/input endpoints to that local edge; they do not persist sample, location, observation or custody data. The edge synchronizes to Wrenfell central integration, which verifies the custody sequence and alone submits queued batches to Drakemoor through a maximum of two acknowledgement-held sessions.\nPlanning baseline\nDecision use\nFramework / start\nFour years; sampling starts 3 February 2027\nFirst wave\n12 crews, 18 sites; moves every 3–10 days\nCoverage\n14 sites no dependable cellular; 4 intermittent\nFleet\n20 managed tablets; 28 BYOD Android/iPad, not MDM-enrollable\nLab boundary\nAt most 2 concurrent external API sessions; held until batch acknowledgement\nDelivery envelope\n2 integration engineers × 6 weeks; security architect 8 review days; freeze 21 Dec–5 Jan\n\nScope, terms and decision criteria\nScope is field capture, custody evidence, synchronization and accepted Drakemoor receipt. Procurement and commercial renegotiation are excluded. “Operationally offline” means the crew can capture and record custody for a full shift without WAN. It does not mean data may be stored on a tablet. Under the schedule, persistent disconnected records can exist only on approved managed infrastructure, encrypted and access-logged.\nDecision criteria: preserve field work under WAN loss; keep end-user devices out of the persistence trust boundary; produce inquiry-ready, attributable custody evidence; obey the two-session laboratory agreement; and fit configuration/integration inside the stated window.\nAccountabilities\nAccountable\nDecision / delivery responsibility\nMalachy Oyelaran\nArchitecture approval, assurance gates and readiness decision\nChidi Macaulay\nField workflow acceptance and pilot custody rehearsal\nMaren Guerrero\nEvidence, time and integrity assurance review\nKamil Dimitrov\nDrakemoor interface coordination and acknowledgement test\nCecilia Gasparyan\nProgramme sponsor; receives readiness outcome\nIntegration engineers\nConfiguration, central integration, queue and test evidence\n\n\n\nSelected target: managed-edge, browser-only field capture\nOne architecture is selected. Each active field location uses the existing approved managed-edge image as managed infrastructure. Its encrypted, access-logged local store is the only disconnected store. The current browser field form is configured to use the edge over a local field network; neither managed tablets nor BYOD are a system of record, custody authority or privileged trust anchor.\nUNMANAGED / END-USER ZONE\nMANAGED FIELD INFRASTRUCTURE\nWRENFELL CENTRAL SERVICES\nDRAKEMOOR SUPPLY\n20 managed tablets + 28 BYOD\nBrowser input only\nNo durable sample/custody data\nNo master keys\nApproved managed-edge image\nEncrypted access-logged store\nValidation • controlled time\nappend-only signed/hash-chained ledger\nSync verifier + central anchor\nConflict/quarantine store\nDurable lab queue\nTwo bounded session workers\nExternal API\nQueued batch submission\nStatus retrieval\nBatch acknowledgement\n\nFlow: ① actor signs into a short-lived browser session and scans/enters the stable sample identifier; ② browser sends over protected local transport to the edge; ③ edge locally accepts or rejects the record, creates custody evidence and returns status; ④ when WAN exists, edge sends idempotently to central verification; ⑤ central queues a lab batch; ⑥ one of at most two workers keeps its session allocated until acknowledgement, then records accepted receipt/status. All network boundaries use protected transport.\nEndpoint, identity and trust controls\nBrowser configuration must disable application cache/offline storage, downloads/exports, form autofill and saved credentials; prevent persistence of temporary files where the browser/OS permits; clear session data on logout; and be validated by device inspection on managed and BYOD representatives. The edge must reject any client mode that needs local durable storage.\nBYOD is confined to an unprivileged browser role. It receives only the active screen and short-lived session; no reusable integration credential, edge administration capability, signing material or custody authority is resident solely on it. A separate authenticated actor action is recorded for each custody event; reauthenticate after configured inactivity, handover, or a device loss report.\nEdge, central service and laboratory integration use distinct least-privilege service identities. Encryption at rest applies to the edge, central evidence store, conflict/quarantine store and laboratory queue. Keys/credentials are provisioned to managed components, rotated/replaced and revocable; loss triggers revocation and recovery without putting master secrets on BYOD.\n\n\nRecord, custody and time design\nObject\nRequired content and control\nSample\nStable capture identifier (barcode/label scan where available); never renumber at a boundary. Required field checks and format/range checks occur at edge before local acceptance.\nCustody event\nStable event ID; sample ID; authenticated responsible actor; action or transfer; UTC timestamp to one-second resolution; prior-event reference; integrity value; time-confidence indicator.\nImmediate vs central validation\nEdge enforces required fields, permitted actions, ID shape and workflow completeness. Central checks cross-site/duplicate/conflicting states and lab schema readiness. Incomplete or central-invalid records cannot silently progress: retain in exception/quarantine with owner.\nLifecycle\nLocally accepted → centrally synchronized/verified → submitted to lab → acknowledged by lab. Rejected, conflict and acknowledgement-unavailable states remain visible—not reported as success.\n\nTrusted time and tamper evidence\nThe managed edge—not the tablet clock—stamps event time in UTC at one-second resolution. Before departure and whenever service is available, the edge obtains a controlled time reference and records its time-confidence/drift state. During a WAN outage it continues from that controlled local basis; if confidence is degraded or a permitted drift bound is exceeded, it records the uncertainty, alerts the field workflow and requires controlled reconciliation. Upload time never replaces original event time.\nBefore reconnection, the edge appends each custody event to an append-only sequence linked to its predecessor and protected with edge-held signing/integrity material. Central synchronization verifies sequence continuity and integrity, retains the original history, and independently anchors the verified sequence/receipt. This makes later alteration or deletion detectable; it does not prove an observation was true, physical possession occurred as asserted, or an authorized actor did not misuse credentials.\nSynchronization, conflicts and evidence export\nEach sample, event and synchronization envelope has a stable identifier. The edge retries safely after a timeout; central acknowledgement is keyed to those identifiers so retransmission converges on one logical sample and one custody sequence. Non-mergeable versions, sequence breaks, rejected updates and schema failures preserve both evidence and enter controlled conflict/quarantine resolution—never last-write-wins. Evidence export packages the custody history, integrity/anchor verification, sync receipts, time-confidence records, laboratory statuses and exception-resolution trail for inquiry without relying on a field device.\n\n\nLaboratory control and operational failure behavior\nCentral integration owns every Drakemoor connection. A durable central queue applies back-pressure so accepted field records remain controlled while the laboratory is slow or unavailable. Exactly two bounded workers may hold external sessions; a worker does not release capacity when it transmits a batch, only when the matching acknowledgement/status settles the batch. This topology prevents 12 crews from creating concurrent external API sessions.\nCondition\nRequired behavior / accountable owner\nNo WAN through full shift\nEdge continues local acceptance, ledger and local status; browser remains non-persistent. Synchronization waits. Field lead follows custody procedure.\nWAN returns / sync timeout\nRetry idempotently; central verifies sequence and anchors it. Uncertain receipt stays “locally accepted” or “synchronized” until confirmed. Integration engineers monitor exceptions.\nDelayed/unavailable lab acknowledgement\nBatch remains queued/submitted; its worker session stays occupied until acknowledgement. Other work is buffered, never represented as accepted. Kamil coordinates interface response.\nLab schema/validation failure\nCentral retains payload and receipt evidence in quarantine; named integration engineer resolves with Kamil, then submits a controlled corrected/referenced update.\nIntegrity, time-confidence or conflict failure\nStop automatic progression; preserve original sequence and exception evidence. Maren directs evidence disposition; Malachy accepts/rejects readiness impact.\nLost BYOD or crew handover\nRevoke browser session; actor reauthenticates on an available endpoint. No durable field record or master secret is recovered from the BYOD device.\n\nProjected outcomes and explicit limits\nUnder the supplied coverage assumption, a crew can record a normal full disconnected shift at the managed edge, later obtaining verified central synchronization and then a distinct laboratory-acknowledged state. Under prolonged WAN loss, records remain locally accepted and inquiry evidence remains at the managed edge; central/lab status is delayed, not inferred. Under delayed lab acknowledgement, the central queue retains work and the two occupied sessions constrain dispatch; field capture continues independently. These are behavioral projections, not throughput or availability commitments.\nUncertainty to resolve during assurance: permitted managed-edge time-confidence/drift threshold, browser controls achievable on the actual BYOD variants, and Drakemoor’s exact response/error examples. These are validation inputs, not alternate target architectures or vendor commitments.\n\n\nDesigns set aside — not part of the target\nSet-aside design\nWhy it is not carried forward\nCloud-only browser capture\nRequires live WAN to record the field event; incompatible with 14 sites without dependable coverage, four intermittent sites, and full-shift disconnected operation.\nDirect tablet-to-lab / per-crew connections\nCecilia’s simplification preference conflicts with a limit of two concurrent external API sessions for 12 crews. Sessions remain occupied until batch acknowledgement, so individual connections are neither licensed nor controllable.\nOffline-first native tablet application with durable local store\nConflicts with the policy prohibition on identifiers, locations, observations and custody records at rest on end-user devices. A new native cross-platform build is also estimated beyond available delivery capacity before validation.\nTreating BYOD as managed infrastructure\nBYOD cannot join Wrenfell MDM and cannot inherit privileged trust, durable storage or custody authority.\nCentral receipt as the only custody evidence\nA later receipt cannot explain disconnected possession or preserve event-time integrity during the outage.\n\nConstraint-to-target trace\nMaterial source constraint\nTarget response / stated limitation\nWAN loss; full shift\nManaged edge holds encrypted records and ledger; endpoint browser does not.\nNo data at rest on end-user device\nPersistence only in approved edge/central stores; cache/download/autofill/temp-path controls tested.\nMixed 20 managed / 28 BYOD fleet\nAll are browser endpoints; BYOD has unprivileged, short-lived access only.\nCustody/inquiry and UTC seconds\nEdge creates actor/action/sample/time evidence, chained before WAN; time confidence is recorded.\nTwo lab sessions, acknowledgement-held\nCentral durable queue and no-more-than-two workers; submitted is distinct from acknowledged.\n12 engineer-weeks; 8 review days; freeze\nConfigure existing browser form and approved edge, then integrate/test around freeze; no greenfield native build.\nLimit of integrity claim\nDetects sequence alteration/deletion; does not prove factual truth or prevent authorised credential misuse.\n\n\n\nDelivery roadmap to mobilisation\nThe roadmap consumes no more than the available 12 integration engineer-weeks and eight security-architect review days. Dates below are planning dates derived from the fixed freeze and 3 February 2027 start; production change is not scheduled during the freeze.\nDates\nMilestone / dependency\nAccountable owner\nCapacity / exit evidence\n14–18 Dec\nAssurance baseline and target specification; precedes configuration\nMalachy; Maren\nSecurity review days 1–2; target/control trace agreed\n21 Dec–5 Jan\nChange freeze: no production change; prepare review material only\nMalachy\nNo deployment; open decisions logged for post-freeze\n6–15 Jan\nConfigure browser form + approved managed edge; define IDs, ledger/time fields and local checks\nIntegration engineers\n4 engineer-weeks; configured build ready for integration\n11–20 Jan\nBuild central verification, idempotent sync, queue and two-session lifecycle; depends on field contract\nIntegration engineers; Kamil\n5 engineer-weeks; interface/status evidence\n18–22 Jan\nSecurity/evidence review and remediate; depends on configured controls\nMaren\nSecurity review days 3–8; cache/storage, keys, time, ledger review\n25–27 Jan\nBounded field pilot/rehearsal; depends on integration and review\nChidi; Maren\n2 engineer-weeks support; pass evidence and field sign-off\n28–29 Jan\nReadiness / go-no-go and controlled release; depends on pilot\nMalachy; Cecilia\n1 engineer-week support; readiness before 3 Feb\n1–2 Feb\nCrew briefing, operational handover and mobilisation check\nChidi; Malachy\nNo feature change; custody fallback confirmed\n\nPilot acceptance and fallback\nProve in rehearsal\nObservable pass evidence\nDisconnected capture / no tablet persistence\nFull-shift WAN simulation accepts records at edge; device inspection finds no sample/custody data in browser cache, downloads, autofill, app/temp paths or saved credentials.\nAttribution, time and integrity\nActor/action/sample/UTC-second fields present; tablet clock change does not set authority; time-confidence degradation is visible; alteration/deletion breaks verification before reconnection.\nRetry, conflict and lab boundary\nTimeout retry creates no duplicate sample/event; non-mergeable update quarantines; concurrent dispatch never exceeds two acknowledgement-held sessions; delayed acknowledgement remains visible.\nGo/no-go and fallback\nGo only when all evidence passes and Malachy accepts. If not, do not release the target: use controlled existing custody procedure and retain paper/physical custody evidence for later controlled entry, preserving original dates/actors and exception record; no unverified electronic custody claim is substituted.\n\n\n\nAssurance release statement\nThis decision deliberately uses available, approved components: configuration of the current browser field form and approved managed-edge image, plus bounded central integration. It does not assume a new product, a new vendor commitment, an extra laboratory connection, a universal device-management capability, or an unsupported performance figure. Readiness is conditional on the pilot evidence and the named reviews; the selected target does not conceal failure states behind upload or lab-submission timestamps.\nArchitecture decision\nFor the Sheafield Mutual first-wave baseline of 12 crews across 18 sites, Wrenfell will implement the managed-edge, browser-only field-data architecture described here: the approved managed edge is the encrypted, access-logged disconnected system of record and creates controlled, tamper-evident custody evidence; tablets, including BYOD, remain non-persistent unprivileged endpoints; Wrenfell central integration verifies and anchors synchronization and queues laboratory traffic through no more than two acknowledgement-held Drakemoor API sessions. This is the single target architecture for mobilisation readiness before field sampling on 3 February 2027.","truncated":false,"note":"DOCX 正文与表格单元格文字提取；不还原分页、字体与版式。","embedded_image_count":0,"embedded_images":[]}}],"rubric":[{"rubric_item_id":"R01","check":"file_introspection","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The required deliverable is a valid, openable DOCX named exactly `target_field_data_architecture.docx`.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":true,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Submitted file metadata and package structure.","grader_hint":"Confirm the exact filename, extension and that the DOCX package opens without repair."},{"rubric_item_id":"R02","check":"geometry","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The rendered document is no more than eight pages, counting diagrams, tables and appendices.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Rendered page count.","grader_hint":"Render the DOCX using a standard office renderer and count every page."},{"rubric_item_id":"R03","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The document is framed as a decision and delivery artifact for Malachy Oyelaran and technical assurance peers rather than as a transcript or general technology essay.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Purpose statement, document structure and tone.","grader_hint":"Look for an immediately identifiable decision, scope and implementation orientation suitable for peer assurance."},{"rubric_item_id":"R04","check":"constraint_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document makes exactly one target architecture recommendation and does not leave two or more alternatives tied or conditionally co-recommended.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Recommendation and conclusion language.","grader_hint":"Count architectures presented as the selected target. Variants within one architecture are acceptable only if they do not create competing target states."},{"rubric_item_id":"R05","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The final substantive passage ends on an unambiguous architecture decision rather than questions, a generic next-step list or a request for someone else to choose.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Final section and final paragraph.","grader_hint":"Read the ending in isolation and verify that a reviewer can state what was decided."},{"rubric_item_id":"R06","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"Before relying on the word “offline,” the document pins down its operational meaning and distinguishes that meaning from absence of persistent sample data on an end-user device.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Definitions, assumptions or architecture narrative.","grader_hint":"Accept equivalent terminology, but the text must remove the ambiguity between operating without WAN access and storing records on a tablet."},{"rubric_item_id":"R07","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The selected architecture supports field capture and custody recording during a full WAN outage while still preventing sample and custody records from persisting at rest on managed or personally owned tablets.","weight":5.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Selected data flow, component responsibilities and disconnected-state behavior.","grader_hint":"Trace a record created with no WAN. It must leave or avoid durable tablet storage and remain available to the field workflow through a compliant architecture component."},{"rubric_item_id":"R08","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Any component that persistently holds disconnected sample data is explicitly treated as approved managed infrastructure rather than as an end-user device.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Trust-boundary diagram and component descriptions.","grader_hint":"Verify that persistent local storage is assigned only to a managed component with a defensible policy classification."},{"rubric_item_id":"R09","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The architecture makes the no-data-at-rest requirement credible for browser and operating-system behavior, addressing local caches, downloads, autofill, temporary files or equivalent persistence paths rather than relying only on a policy statement.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Endpoint controls and validation requirements.","grader_hint":"Look for concrete technical treatment of common client-side persistence channels and a way to verify the treatment."},{"rubric_item_id":"R10","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Personally owned devices are confined to a role that does not require MDM enrollment, privileged trust or classification as managed infrastructure.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"BYOD trust model and access design.","grader_hint":"Trace the privileges and data exposure of a BYOD tablet; it must not silently inherit controls available only on Wrenfell-managed tablets."},{"rubric_item_id":"R11","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The design includes proportionate session, authentication and reauthentication behavior for shared field conditions without placing reusable secrets or custody authority solely on a BYOD device.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Identity and session-control description.","grader_hint":"Assess whether actor attribution remains meaningful when devices are shared, lost or used through a disconnected shift."},{"rubric_item_id":"R12","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"Sample identity is maintained through capture, custody events and laboratory submission using a stable identifier and a practical mechanism such as barcode or label scanning, without unsupported renumbering.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Logical data flow and identity controls.","grader_hint":"Follow one sample across every boundary and verify that its identity can be reconciled without manual reinterpretation."},{"rubric_item_id":"R13","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"Field validation distinguishes data that must be checked immediately from validation that depends on laboratory or central services, with invalid or incomplete records prevented from silently progressing.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Validation and exception handling.","grader_hint":"Look for defensible validation under disconnection and explicit handling of records that cannot yet pass central checks."},{"rubric_item_id":"R14","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"Each custody event preserves, at minimum, the sample identity, responsible actor, custody action or transfer, and event time.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Custody event model or architecture narrative.","grader_hint":"Verify that the event content can answer who had what, what changed and when."},{"rubric_item_id":"R15","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Custody event times are represented in UTC at the source-required one-second resolution throughout the selected flow.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Timestamp specification and data-flow annotations.","grader_hint":"Confirm both UTC and one-second resolution and check that later transformations do not weaken them."},{"rubric_item_id":"R16","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The timestamp design does not treat a user-adjustable tablet clock as authoritative.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Trusted-time design and custody-event creation flow.","grader_hint":"The authoritative event time must come from, or be defensibly related to, a controlled source rather than merely copying the tablet clock."},{"rubric_item_id":"R17","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The architecture addresses clock drift and loss of an external time service during an outage, including how uncertainty or correction is recorded rather than concealed.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Time-control, monitoring and reconciliation description.","grader_hint":"Look for a controlled local time basis, drift detection or bounds, and an auditable response when time confidence is degraded."},{"rubric_item_id":"R18","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"Disconnected custody events are made tamper-evident before WAN reconnection using an append-only, chained, signed or equivalently defensible integrity mechanism.","weight":5.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Custody ledger or integrity-control description.","grader_hint":"The mechanism must expose alteration or deletion of offline events; ordinary database permissions alone are insufficient."},{"rubric_item_id":"R19","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The document accurately limits what tamper evidence proves and does not equate it with proof that an observation was true or that no authorized person misused credentials.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Assurance claims and limitations.","grader_hint":"Credit explicit, technically accurate boundaries around integrity, identity and factual accuracy."},{"rubric_item_id":"R20","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"Reconnection provides an independent central anchor, receipt or verification of the disconnected integrity sequence while preserving the original event history.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Synchronization and verification flow.","grader_hint":"Confirm that reconnection verifies and anchors existing events rather than replacing their times with upload times."},{"rubric_item_id":"R21","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The synchronization design is retry-safe and prevents retransmission from creating duplicate samples or duplicate custody events.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Identifiers, acknowledgements and retry behavior.","grader_hint":"Trace a timeout after submission but before receipt of an acknowledgement; a retry must converge on one logical record."},{"rubric_item_id":"R22","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"Conflicting versions or rejected updates are surfaced for controlled resolution and are not silently resolved by last-write-wins behavior.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Conflict and exception handling.","grader_hint":"Look for preservation of both evidence and an accountable resolution path when two states cannot be merged automatically."},{"rubric_item_id":"R23","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Laboratory traffic is consolidated so that the selected architecture cannot exceed Drakemoor Supply’s limit of two concurrent external API sessions.","weight":5.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Integration topology and connection-management description.","grader_hint":"Trace all twelve crews to the lab boundary and verify that concurrency is centrally bounded at two or fewer sessions."},{"rubric_item_id":"R24","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The architecture accounts for a laboratory session remaining occupied until its submitted batch is acknowledged.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Queue, worker or session lifecycle.","grader_hint":"Verify that capacity is not calculated as if a session became free immediately after transmission."},{"rubric_item_id":"R25","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"A durable queue or equivalent buffer controls laboratory back-pressure without blocking disconnected field capture or losing accepted field records.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Integration component responsibilities and failure flow.","grader_hint":"Assess behavior during slow acknowledgements or an unavailable lab interface; field records must remain controlled and recoverable."},{"rubric_item_id":"R26","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The design distinguishes locally accepted, centrally synchronized, submitted-to-lab and acknowledged-by-lab states or equivalent lifecycle states.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Status model and operational flow.","grader_hint":"A user or operator must be able to tell whether a record merely exists locally or has actually been accepted by Drakemoor."},{"rubric_item_id":"R27","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"Laboratory validation failures, schema errors and unavailable acknowledgements enter visible retry or quarantine handling with ownership, rather than disappearing or being reported as successful.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Exception paths and roadmap ownership.","grader_hint":"Look for a controlled error state, retained evidence and a named role responsible for resolution."},{"rubric_item_id":"R28","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"Access between the field client, managed local component, central integration service and laboratory is least-privileged and separated by role.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Trust boundaries, identities and authorization description.","grader_hint":"Verify that compromise of one tablet or crew account does not automatically grant administration of the local store or laboratory integration."},{"rubric_item_id":"R29","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"Persistent sample and custody data is encrypted at rest in each managed storage location and protected in transit across each network boundary.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Security controls mapped to stores and flows.","grader_hint":"Check every persistent store and network hop, not just the central platform."},{"rubric_item_id":"R30","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"Key and credential handling addresses provisioning, rotation or replacement, revocation and recovery without placing master secrets on BYOD tablets.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Key-management and operational-control description.","grader_hint":"Credit a proportionate lifecycle; reject designs whose integrity becomes permanently unverifiable after one device or credential is lost."},{"rubric_item_id":"R31","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"Audit evidence can be exported as a coherent package for a planning inquiry, including custody history, integrity verification and relevant synchronization or exception evidence.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Audit and evidence-export design.","grader_hint":"Assess whether a reviewer could reconstruct the chain without direct access to a field device or undocumented operator knowledge."},{"rubric_item_id":"R32","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The document includes a legible end-to-end architecture or data-flow view covering field entry, disconnected persistence, synchronization, central integration and accepted laboratory receipt.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Rendered architecture diagram or equivalent visual.","grader_hint":"The visual must show components and directional flows clearly enough to trace one sample from capture through acknowledgement."},{"rubric_item_id":"R33","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The architecture view marks meaningful trust or management boundaries, including the distinction between BYOD, managed field infrastructure, Wrenfell central services and Drakemoor Supply.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Architecture visual and legend.","grader_hint":"Accept explicit boundary lines, zones or clearly labeled component ownership if the separation is unambiguous."},{"rubric_item_id":"R34","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"A traceable decision record maps every material source constraint to a feature of the selected architecture or to an explicit limitation.","weight":5.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Decision rationale, constraint mapping or assurance table.","grader_hint":"Check coverage of WAN loss, no tablet data at rest, mixed BYOD, custody integrity and time, two lab sessions, delivery capacity and mobilisation timing."},{"rubric_item_id":"R35","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The document expressly sets aside direct per-crew or per-tablet laboratory connections because they conflict with licensed concurrency and controllable integration behavior.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Designs-set-aside discussion.","grader_hint":"The rejected design should be recognizable and the reason should follow from the two-session source constraint, not preference."},{"rubric_item_id":"R36","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The document sets aside cloud-only field capture as incapable of supporting the confirmed coverage conditions.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Designs-set-aside discussion.","grader_hint":"Look for explicit rejection of a design that requires live WAN access to record the field event."},{"rubric_item_id":"R37","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The document sets aside durable native storage on tablets, including an otherwise conventional offline-first application, because it conflicts with the end-user-device restriction and delivery capacity.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Designs-set-aside discussion.","grader_hint":"The rationale must connect both policy and the source estimate showing a new native cross-platform application is outside the available implementation window."},{"rubric_item_id":"R38","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The selected approach is achievable within two integration engineers for six weeks each and eight security-architect review days, rather than assuming the textbook greenfield build that the source rules out.","weight":5.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Effort allocation, reuse assumptions and roadmap.","grader_hint":"Review the plan for credible reuse of the supplied existing capabilities and ensure assigned work does not exceed the stated capacities."},{"rubric_item_id":"R39","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The roadmap sequences architecture assurance, configuration or implementation, integration, security review, field validation, readiness and mobilisation in a dependency-aware order.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Roadmap dates, dependencies and milestones.","grader_hint":"Check that predecessor work is complete before dependent testing or release; labels alone are insufficient."},{"rubric_item_id":"R40","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Roadmap dates respect the 21 December through 5 January change freeze and culminate in readiness before field sampling begins on 3 February 2027.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Dated roadmap and milestone logic.","grader_hint":"No production change should be scheduled inside the freeze unless the source explicitly permits it, and readiness must precede 3 February."},{"rubric_item_id":"R41","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"Every material roadmap activity or milestone has a concrete accountable owner drawn from a source-supported role or named stakeholder.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Roadmap ownership fields.","grader_hint":"Reject vague ownership such as “team” where accountability for assurance, integration, field acceptance or lab coordination would remain unclear."},{"rubric_item_id":"R42","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"Acceptance testing covers disconnected capture, absence of tablet persistence, custody attribution, trusted-time degradation, tamper detection, retry deduplication and the two-session laboratory limit.","weight":5.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Test and acceptance portion of the roadmap.","grader_hint":"Each named risk must have observable pass or fail evidence; a generic system-test milestone does not satisfy this item."},{"rubric_item_id":"R43","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The plan includes a bounded field pilot or rehearsal and an actionable fallback or rollback position that preserves custody evidence if readiness criteria are not met.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Pilot, cutover and contingency plan.","grader_hint":"Look for a defined go/no-go basis and a safe response, not simply an instruction to investigate failures."},{"rubric_item_id":"R44","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"Projected future outcomes are tied to explicit source-supported assumptions and include at least the normal disconnected operating case and a degraded case such as prolonged WAN loss or delayed lab acknowledgement.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Outcome projection, scenario or assurance narrative.","grader_hint":"The outcomes should describe architecture behavior and operational consequences without inventing percentages or unsupported throughput."},{"rubric_item_id":"R45","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The chosen numerical planning baseline is internally consistent across the recommendation, diagram, capacity discussion and roadmap, including twelve crews, eighteen first-wave sites, the fleet figures, two lab sessions and the stated delivery capacity wherever those figures are used.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"All numerical statements in the document.","grader_hint":"Cross-check repeated figures and any derived allocations. Allow omission where a figure is irrelevant, but not contradictory scale assumptions."},{"rubric_item_id":"R46","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The rendered document is coherent, legible and executable under the page cap: sequencing is easy to follow, diagrams and tables remain readable, and a peer reviewer can identify the decision, controls, ownership and timing without asking the author to interpret it.","weight":5.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Whole rendered document.","grader_hint":"Judge professional usability as a whole, including hierarchy, transitions, visual legibility and whether critical qualifications are discoverable."},{"rubric_item_id":"R47","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The document selects, recommends or implies endorsement of a new named product, or presents an unsupported cost, staffing level, throughput, availability percentage, retention period, interface capability or vendor commitment as fact.","weight":-8.0,"polarity":"penalty","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Product names, quantitative claims and vendor assertions throughout the document.","grader_hint":"Apply once if any prohibited invented commitment or figure is used to support the decision. Generic component types and clearly labeled non-numeric uncertainties are not violations."}],"spec":{"professional_context":"Wrenfell Geoenvironmental is mobilising a multi-year remediation programme whose disconnected field operations, custody obligations, mixed device estate and licensed laboratory interface require a bounded architecture decision. The document will be challenged by Malachy Oyelaran and technical assurance peers before implementation begins.","difficulty_sources":["The word “offline” has two materially different stakeholder meanings that must be defined before it can support a decision.","Disconnected operation and a prohibition on sample data at rest on end-user devices must be reconciled through a defensible trust boundary.","Tamper-evident custody timestamps must survive loss of WAN and trusted external time without overstating what integrity evidence proves.","A two-session laboratory licence conflicts with per-crew integration at twelve-crew scale.","Mixed BYOD prevents reliance on uniform endpoint management.","Limited engineering capacity and a change freeze make a greenfield native application infeasible, requiring a supportable fallback using available capabilities.","The deliverable must transform correspondence into a single architecture, dependent control model, projected scenarios and executable roadmap under an eight-page cap."],"authoring_plan":{"reference_file":{"filename":"field_architecture_correspondence.pdf","form":"A concise, internally consistent collection of dated emails and message excerpts from the named stakeholders, with quoted policy text and confirmed technical facts distinguishable from preferences.","required_facts":["Wrenfell Geoenvironmental has a four-year remediation framework for Sheafield Mutual, with field sampling beginning 3 February 2027.","The first wave has twelve crews and eighteen sites; crews relocate every three to ten days.","Fourteen sites have no dependable cellular coverage and four have intermittent service; crews must operate through a full disconnected shift.","The field team uses “offline” to mean no WAN dependency, while security discussion sometimes uses it to mean no persistent sample data on the tablet. The source must expose both usages without resolving the architecture.","Sheafield policy prohibits sample identifiers, locations, observations and custody records at rest on an end-user device. A clarification allows persistence in approved managed infrastructure when encrypted and access-logged.","The fleet contains twenty managed tablets and twenty-eight personally owned Android and iPad devices. BYOD devices cannot be enrolled in Wrenfell MDM.","The current browser-based field form and an existing approved managed-edge image are available for configuration; the source must not name a commercial edge product.","Drakemoor Supply’s laboratory interface allows no more than two concurrent external API sessions. A session remains occupied until the submitted batch is acknowledged. Queued batch submission and status retrieval are supported.","Cecilia Gasparyan prefers direct tablet-to-lab submission as a simplification, but her statement is a preference rather than a controlling requirement.","Custody evidence may be produced in a planning inquiry. Every event needs sample identity, actor, action or transfer and event time.","Sheafield requires UTC event times at one-second resolution. Users can alter tablet clocks, and no external time service is guaranteed during a site outage.","Two integration engineers are available for six weeks each and the security architect for eight review days before mobilisation.","A new native cross-platform capture application is estimated beyond the available capacity even before validation. Configuration of the current browser form and approved managed-edge image fits within the available window if the architecture uses them defensibly.","A change freeze runs from 21 December through 5 January.","No source-supported costs, throughput rates, service availability percentages, retention periods or additional vendor commitments are provided."],"imperfections":["Use the ambiguous term “offline” in stakeholder messages with the two ordinary meanings, requiring the author to define it in the decision document.","Include a senior stakeholder preference for direct tablet-to-lab integration even though the confirmed two-session licensing and acknowledgement behavior makes that topology unsuitable at twelve-crew scale.","Present the no-at-rest rule and disconnected-working need in separate messages so the practitioner must identify the managed local infrastructure boundary that reconciles them.","Present resource estimates and the freeze dates in different messages so roadmap feasibility requires synthesis rather than copying one schedule."]},"reference_solution":{"intended_verdict":"Recommend one architecture in which managed field infrastructure, not the tablet, is the disconnected system of record. Tablets use a tightly controlled browser session over a local field network without durable sample storage. The managed field component validates and persists records, supplies controlled event time, appends tamper-evident custody events and later synchronizes them to a central integration service. The central service verifies and anchors custody evidence, queues idempotent laboratory batches and limits workers to no more than two acknowledged-session lifecycles at once.","required_reasoning":["Define operational offline working separately from tablet data persistence.","Treat BYOD clients as untrusted presentation and input endpoints, not managed storage or custody authorities.","Use stable sample and event identifiers, retry-safe synchronization, explicit lifecycle states and controlled conflict handling.","Use a managed time source at the field component, record drift or uncertainty and avoid claiming that tamper evidence proves the truth of an observation.","Consolidate all laboratory submissions behind centrally bounded queue workers because per-crew connections violate the licensed concurrency model.","Reject cloud-only capture, direct per-crew lab submission and durable native tablet storage for distinct source-grounded reasons.","Prefer configuration and integration of available capabilities over a greenfield native application because the available engineering and security-review capacity makes the textbook build infeasible.","Sequence assurance, configuration, integration, testing, pilot and readiness around the freeze and before 3 February 2027."],"gold_relationships":["Every architecture component that stores sample data must be inside a managed trust boundary and must have encryption and access logging.","Every custody event must preserve sample identity, actor, action and UTC time at one-second resolution.","The authoritative custody time must not be derived solely from a user-adjustable tablet clock.","A retransmitted logical record must converge on one sample and one event sequence rather than create duplicates.","Laboratory concurrency must never exceed two sessions, and each session remains allocated until acknowledgement.","The roadmap workload assigned to integration engineers must fit within a total of twelve engineer-weeks, and security review must fit within eight days.","No production change may be planned during 21 December through 5 January, and operational readiness must precede 3 February 2027.","Projected outcomes must be conditional on supplied assumptions and must not introduce unsupported quantitative performance claims."],"space_feasibility":"An eight-page document can accommodate a concise decision summary, one architecture and trust-boundary diagram, a constraint-to-design table, a short designs-set-aside section, controls and failure behavior, a roadmap with owners and dates, acceptance criteria, scenarios and a final decision. The rubric does not require separate prose sections for every control; tables and diagram annotations may satisfy multiple distinct checks without exceeding the cap."}},"design_constraints":{"artifact_scope":"primary_plus_appendix","structure":"strict_schema"},"reference_files":[{"artifact_id":"ref_field_architecture_correspondence","filename":"field_architecture_correspondence.pdf","purpose":"Consolidated stakeholder correspondence containing the controlling policy language, field conditions, fleet facts, laboratory interface constraints, resource estimates, dates and stakeholder positions for the architecture decision.","required":true,"discovery":"Read the correspondence as the complete factual record; distinguish quoted policy and confirmed technical facts from stakeholder preferences."}],"deliverables":[{"deliverable_id":"target_architecture_document","filename":"target_field_data_architecture.docx","media_type":"application/vnd.openxmlformats-officedocument.wordprocessingml.document","description":"A peer-reviewable target field-data architecture decision and executable implementation roadmap covering field capture through accepted laboratory receipt.","required":true,"constraints":["Top-level working-directory file; maximum eight pages including diagrams, tables and appendices; exactly one recommended architecture; rejected designs visibly separate; one consistent numerical planning baseline; no invented figures or new named-product selection; conclusion ends on the decision."],"rendering_required":true}],"work_activity":"make_decisions","work_product":"project_plan","internet_policy":"forbidden","evidence_policy":"professional_judgment"},"source_metadata":{"prompt_version":"authored_doc@v4","occupation":"computer_information_systems_managers","sector":"professional_scientific_technical_services","task_archetype":"authored_doc","estimated_professional_hours":10.0,"gold_policy_self_report_not_verified":{"author_type":"hybrid","reviewer_policy":"The reference solution should be produced by an information-systems architecture practitioner and independently reviewed by one field-data or integration specialist and one security or digital-evidence specialist. Reviewers must trace a sample through disconnected capture, custody integrity, synchronization and laboratory acknowledgement, then verify roadmap feasibility against the supplied capacity and dates.","minimum_reviewers":2,"known_limitations":["Several implementation-level mechanisms can satisfy the required controls, so grading should accept technically equivalent managed-edge, trusted-time, tamper-evidence and queueing designs. Product-specific implementation details are intentionally neither supplied nor required."],"reference_solution_passed_rubric":true}},"validation":{"label":"结构/材料验收记录","detail":"validated 属于结构、防泄漏、材料推进验收；没有内容正确率结论。","formal":false,"release_ready_recorded":true,"checks":[{"check":"package_integrity","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-25T09:08:14+00:00"},{"check":"gold_isolation","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-25T09:08:14+00:00"},{"check":"artifact_format","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-25T09:08:14+00:00"},{"check":"rendering","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-25T09:08:14+00:00"},{"check":"instruction_rubric_overlap","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":false,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-25T09:08:14+00:00"}]},"source_path":"/inspire/hdd2/project/special-project-1/public/ynan/gdpval_data/corpus_c3/runs/1159e323a743a6f8","declared_deliverables":["target_field_data_architecture.docx"]};
